  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 148 PM EDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nA pretty classic looking Omega blocking pattern will set up across \nthe continental United States for the back half of the Labor Day \nweekend. An anomalously strong upper level low pressure system over \nthe Pacific Northwest will finally begin to push into the \nIntermountain West by Sunday. This will begin to flatten and \nshift the influence of the Great Plains ridge further to the east \nand closer to Florida. Meanwhile, deep mid level troughing will \nextend south across the eastern seaboard as the main trough axis \npushes pushes offshore into the mid-Atlantic. The passage of this \ntrough will help push a surface cold front south into North \nFlorida by Monday. This trough looks to stop just short of South \nFlorida and stall over the northern part of the peninsula through\nthe middle of the week. However, some guidance is hinting at a \nfew shortwave vorticity impulses digging further south into the \nPeninsula behind the main trough axis, which would likely \ninvigorate storm activity and increase coverage of precipitation \nthroughout the region. The overall wind pattern will remain pretty\nweak, but the surface high over the Atlantic will keep winds more\nsoutherly. As a result, deep moisture will continue to advect \ninto the region through the middle of the week. Guidance continues to\nhint at PWATs remaining above 2 inches, which is above the 75th \npercentile for this time of year. There is an indication that some\ncoastal convergence may develop closer to the stalled front, as \nwinds aloft shift more westerly and southeasterly winds at the \nsurface keep storms pinned to the coast. But with relatively weak\nsteering flow, most convection will generally focus along sea \nbreeze and outflow boundary interactions during the afternoons. \nUnder a moisture rich atmosphere, locally heavy rainfall will be \nthe main threat each afternoon, bringing nuisance urban flooding \nconcerns to low lying and poor drainage areas. \n\nBy the middle of the week, the ridge over the central CONUS will \nexpand eastward into Florida. The surface boundary over north \nFlorida will wash out and surface high pressure over the Atlantic \nwill once again exert greater influence over the peninsula and shift \nwinds easterly again. Moisture content looks to be on the downtrend \nby the end of the week, which will lower chances of precipitation \nacross the region. Scattered storm coverage will likely favor \ninterior and southwest Florida, along sea breeze boundary \ninteractions. Storms will be more run-of-the-mill, with typical \nlocally heavy rainfall, frequent lighting, and gusty winds being the \nmain threats. Temperatures through the period will remain near \nnormal for this time of year, with highs in the upper 80s to low \n90s. Widespread Moderate HeatRisk will continue to impact all urban \nareas, affecting sensitive individuals without proper hydration and \ncooling.",
